One dimensional Array:

Array is a collection of similar type data elements.

declaration:
datatype arrayname[size];
ex: int num[5];

size of array is known as boundary condition of array.

All arrays consist of contiguous memory locations. 
The lowest address corresponds to the first element 
and the highest address to the last element.
That's why Array is a linear data structure.

By using an Array,
we can able to do a same task for more than one same kind of elements.

We can able to access each element by index.
starting index: 0   end index:n-1
n:total no of elements

Initializing Arrays:
You can initialize an array in C either one by one by using loop
or using a single statement as follows −

double balance[5] = {1000.0, 2.0, 3.4, 7.0, 50.0};

Array initialization by loop:
for(i=0;i<n;i++)               //consider num is an user given array
{
scanf("%d",&num[i]);
}

Print elements of Array by using loop:
for(i=0;i<n;i++)
{
printf("%d",num[i]);
}
